contentText:
Turnstile brought the pit to America&#8217;s front yard on Saturday (Oct. 24), playing a free show for more than 60,000 fans gathered near the Washington Monument in Washington, D.C. The Baltimore hardcore group&#8217;s biggest show to date was also the final gig on the North American tour in support of their Never Enough album and a landmark for the group whose Maryland-bred singer is a practical native of the nation&#8217;s capital.

The show, &#8220;Turnstile at the Washington Monument,&#8221; was co-hosted by Human Concern International and aimed at raising funds to benefit humanitarian efforts in Palestine, Sudan and Lebanon.

The gig drew legions of the Grammy-winning hardcore band&#8217;s fans to the hilly lawn near the iconic monument for a rager that featured massive, roiling mosh pits , a likely first for the august site that in the past has hosted everything from the massive 1963 civil rights March on Washington to this past summer&#8217;s tepid Great American State Fair Kickoff.

According to The Washington Post , singer Brendan Yates told the crowd, &#8220;Today we’re here to gather around music to celebrate humanity. But also to fight for it. Be loud enough for the White House to hear,” then leading the crowd in a pro-Palestinian chant, &#8220;Free Palestine!,&#8221; with his head turned toward the White House just blocks away, as well as &#8220;Free Sudan!&#8221; and &#8220;Free Lebanon!&#8221;

Before the 75-minute show, Human Concern International&#8217;s global director announced that the show had reportedly earned $150,000, easily surpassing the initial $100K goal. The 19-track setlist included such beloved songs as &#8220;Birds,&#8221; &#8220;Endless,&#8221; &#8220;Keep It Moving,&#8221; &#8220;Holiday,&#8221; &#8220;Blackout&#8221; and the finale, &#8220;T.L.C. (Turnstile Love Connection).&#8221;

Turnstile won their first of two Grammy Awards in February for best rock album ( Never Enough ) and best metal performance (&#8220;Birds&#8221;) and then made their Saturday Night Live debut earlier this month, becoming the first hardcore group to rock the Studio 8H stage since Fear&#8217;s legendarily chaotic 1981 debut.

